  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Scattered showers and thunderstorms across \nmostly northern and central areas today. Rain, with a slight \nchance of thunderstorms, across the entire region Monday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nA cold front will slowly move east across Quebec province today\nwhile low pressure forms along the front. Although the amount of\ninstability is uncertain, expect isolated/scattered showers and\nthunderstorms will develop across northern and central areas \nthis afternoon then persist into the evening in advance of the \napproaching cold front. The cold front will approach the Saint \nLawrence River Valley later tonight. Showers will occur across \nthe forecast area overnight. The intensifying low will track \nnorthwest, then north, of Maine Monday drawing the cold front \nacross the forecast area. Rain will occur across the entire \nforecast area Monday with the cold front. Although instability \nwill be limited, could also have the slight chance of a \nthunderstorm. Rain then tapers to showers Monday night in the \nwake of the cold front. Models agree that the greater rain \ntotals with this event should occur along the track of the low \nacross Quebec province. However, uncertainty still exists \nregarding rainfall totals across the forecast area dependent on \nthe location and persistence of where areas of heavier rain \nmight develop. Areas of dense fog are also possible this morning\nand again tonight with moisture moving inland from the Gulf of \nMaine. The best chance for dense fog will occur across Downeast \nareas.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Scattered showers and thunderstorms late this \nweek.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nHigh pressure over the area mid week weakens Thursday as upper\nlevel shortwave energy moves in from the west. Models are\ncurrently showing the potential for some weak low pressure\ndevelopment in the area which may kick off some showers and\nstorms. Long range ensemble guidance shows the potential for at\nleast a few hundred J/kg of instability but is relatively\nuncertain in when/where the best forcing for ascent will be so \nwill need to wait to get better clarity on storm location,\nstrength, and coverage.",
